@prefix chg: <https://semblance.dev/ns/change#> .
@prefix mig: <https://semblance.dev/ns/migration#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix change: <https://semblance.dev/change/> .
@prefix c: <https://semblance.dev/ns/component#> .
@prefix sem: <https://semblance.dev/ns/core#> .

# What changed for whoever has to move with it: AGENTS.md, "When a component changes".

change:numeric-rounding a chg:Change ;
    rdfs:label "Numbers are read rounded once to their target, and shapes check numeric lexical forms"@en ;
    chg:issue <https://github.com/inferal-oss/semblance/issues/182> ;
    chg:motivation """Decimal text was not always read as the nearest binary64 or binary32 value: the Rust engine
rounded a wide integer before scaling it, the WAT engine read an xsd:float through a double first (rounding twice),
and the function twins used Number(), which reads 0x10 and a no-break space. A field's minimum and maximum and a
shape's datatype check could then disagree between the engines, and between a SPARQL rule and its twin.""" ;
    chg:substance """The engines (Rust and WAT) and the function twins share one reader (src/numeric.ts on the host):
the XML Schema lexical form after XML white space (space, tab, CR, LF), rounded once, ties to even, to the
target's precision; an xsd:float cast from text or an exact number goes straight to binary32. In the shapes layer
(sh:datatype), a stored literal of any of the sixteen XSD numeric datatypes (integer, decimal, float, double, long,
int, short, byte and the signed and unsigned subtypes) must be in that datatype's lexical form, sign and range, or
it is reported: "128"^^xsd:byte, "-1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger, "1e2"^^xsd:decimal are now violations. The
compatibility of xsd:integer, xsd:decimal, xsd:float and xsd:double values among themselves is unchanged, except
that an infinite or NaN value no longer passes as an xsd:integer. In the validation layer a field's text for
xsd:integer or xsd:decimal must be in its lexical form around XML white space, as before (#337), with a no-break
space now refused by the function twin as by the SPARQL rule, and sh:minInclusive / sh:maxInclusive compare the
text read as xsd:double, so the twin no longer reads "0x10" as 16.""" ;
    chg:forwardGuide """Nothing to move in an app's graph. Data that stated a numeric literal outside its datatype
(a byte of 128, an unsigned value below zero) is now reported by the shapes layer: correct the value or its
datatype. A field that relied on a no-break space around a number being accepted by the function twin trims it
before validation.""" ;
    chg:backwardGuide """Going back, out-of-range or ill-formed subtype literals pass sh:datatype again, xsd:float
is read through a double, and the function twins read numbers with Number(). Nothing in the graph changes.""" ;
    chg:affects sem:Component .
